00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:25.530
 What's up Marvel fans
we're here with Chris McKenna

00:02:25.530 --> 00:02:27.510
and Erik Sommers, the
writers of the film.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.520
Y'all have so much
to juggle in this.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:30.940
There are so many characters.

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:31.740
Where do you even begin?

00:02:36.450 --> 00:02:38.200
 We didn't know where to begin.

00:02:38.200 --> 00:02:41.310
We honestly, we--
after the last movie,

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:43.140
there were a lot
of different things

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.770
that we couldn't
do in a good way.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.530
I think those kind of
limitations are good.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:54.330
But once the idea of the
multiverse opened up to us,

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.820
obviously there was an
infinite number of ways to go

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:02.760
and almost an embarrassment of
riches in terms of what we had

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:06.060
to choose from, but also
restrain ourselves because we

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:09.150
knew we had to tell one story.

00:03:09.150 --> 00:03:11.220
 Yeah, I would just
say that with the other,

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:14.310
with the preceding two
Spider-Man movies, we always

00:03:14.310 --> 00:03:15.780
had to-- and with this one--

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.090
we had some housekeeping
to do dealing

00:03:18.090 --> 00:03:21.900
with the larger MCU and other
things that had happened.

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:25.380
And we had limitations
always, a lot

00:03:25.380 --> 00:03:27.070
of which were fun to deal with.

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:28.570
But in this one in
addition to that,

00:03:28.570 --> 00:03:31.980
we then just had this door open
to the multiverse and just a

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:34.180
truly an infinite
number of possibilities.

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:35.280
And it was almost overwhelming.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.250
And it took us a long time to
just talk and talk about it

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:41.490
and realize, like what is the
most fun we could have with it?

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.020
And hopefully we did.

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:44.280
 And with the
multiverse, we have

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.160
super villains from previous
Spider-Man films in this.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.980
What was it like to write
for those characters?

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:53.520
 Daunting, it was
really daunting.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.120
You know, we had already watched
those movies so many times.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.050
But now we had to really
watch them again and really

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.480
try to live up to,
make sure we're

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:04.590
living up to the characters.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:07.950
But, you know, I mean, Sam
Raimi is a hero of ours

00:04:07.950 --> 00:04:10.540
and Marc Webb is an
incredible filmmaker.

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:15.750
We wanted to do justice to the
worlds that they had created,

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:20.670
but also make sure that they fit
into the world that, you know,

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:22.720
that we've been so
honored to be a part of.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:26.880
So it was a juggling act,
you know, it really was.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:30.450
 It's funny, you know, the
previous Spider-Man movies are

00:04:30.450 --> 00:04:33.180
movies that I would find
myself quoting with friends

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:35.640
or just in a room with
writers discussing

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:36.870
something for reference.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:38.370
And this is the first
time we're suddenly

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:41.760
in my career I was writing
for those characters

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:44.280
that I just thought were like
some kind of amazing reference

00:04:44.280 --> 00:04:45.750
or something I was a fan of.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:47.670
And, yeah, it was
daunting, it was daunting.

00:04:47.670 --> 00:04:49.440
But it was also
really, really fun.

